    counselling: "Perform chest physiotherapy diligently every day — it is as important as medication. Drink plenty of water to keep sputum thin. Watch for signs of exacerbation: change in sputum colour/volume, increased SOB, fever. Complete antibiotics fully.",
    followUp: "Respiratory clinic in 4 weeks. Repeat sputum culture. Consider long-term azithromycin if >3 exacerbations/year. Annual influenza and pneumococcal vaccines. Pulmonary function tests every 6-12 months.",
    pearls: "Pseudomonas aeruginosa is the most common pathogen in advanced bronchiectasis. Mucoid phenotype indicates chronic infection. Ciprofloxacin is the only oral anti-pseudomonal — resistance emerges rapidly with repeated use. Nebulised hypertonic saline improves mucociliary clearance. Long-term macrolides reduce exacerbation frequency via immunomodulation, not just anti-inflammatory effects.",
