    diagnosis: "Acute Infective Exacerbation of Bronchiectasis (Pseudomonas aeruginosa)",
    ddx: ["Bronchiectasis Exacerbation", "COPD Exacerbation", "Pneumonia", "Pulmonary TB Reactivation", "Allergic Bronchopulmonary Aspergillosis"],
    goals: "Eradicate or suppress Pseudomonas, improve sputum clearance, reduce exacerbation frequency, preserve lung function",
    pharm: "1. Antibiotics based on previous sputum cultures: Oral Ciprofloxacin 750mg BID for 14 days (if Pseudomonas sensitive). 2. If resistant or severe exacerbation: consider IV antibiotics — Ceftazidime 2g Q8H or Meropenem 1g Q8H + Tobramycin (if needed). 3. Airway clearance: Nebulised hypertonic saline (7%) BID + chest physiotherapy TID. 4. Bronchodilator: Salbutamol neb 5mg before physiotherapy. 5. If haemoptysis significant: consider bronchial artery embolisation (BAE).",
    nonPharm: "Chest physiotherapy (postural drainage + percussion) TID. Increased fluid intake to thin secretions. Nutritional support: high-protein diet. Pulmonary rehabilitation.",
    carePlan: "Admit for IV antibiotics and intensive chest physiotherapy. Send sputum for culture and sensitivity. Start IV Ceftazidime 2g Q8H + Ciprofloxacin 750mg PO BID. Monitor sputum volume and purulence daily. Consider N-acetylcysteine 600mg BID as mucolytic. If frequent exacerbations (>3/year), consider long-term macrolide therapy (Azithromycin 250mg 3x/week).",
    dtps: "1. Acute infective exacerbation of bronchiectasis with Pseudomonas requiring targeted antibiotic therapy. 2. Chronic sputum production and impaired airway clearance requiring physiotherapy. 3. Risk of disease progression and lung function decline with each exacerbation.",
    monitoring: "Daily: sputum volume and purulence chart, temperature, oxygen saturation. CRP and WBC trend. Pulmonary function tests when stable. Sputum culture at end of antibiotics. Monitor for aminoglycoside toxicity if on Tobramycin.",
    counselling: "Perform chest physiotherapy diligently every day — it is as important as medication. Drink plenty of water to keep sputum thin. Watch for signs of exacerbation: change in sputum colour/volume, increased SOB, fever. Complete antibiotics fully.",
    followUp: "Respiratory clinic in 4 weeks. Repeat sputum culture. Consider long-term azithromycin if >3 exacerbations/year. Annual influenza and pneumococcal vaccines. Pulmonary function tests every 6-12 months.",
    pearls: "Pseudomonas aeruginosa is the most common pathogen in advanced bronchiectasis. Mucoid phenotype indicates chronic infection. Ciprofloxacin is the only oral anti-pseudomonal — resistance emerges rapidly with repeated use. Nebulised hypertonic saline improves mucociliary clearance. Long-term macrolides reduce exacerbation frequency via immunomodulation, not just anti-inflammatory effects.",
